 mb-6\" href=\"/blog\" data-discover=\"true\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-arrow-left w-4 h-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m12 19-7-7 7-7\"></path><path d=\"M19 12H5\"></path></svg> The Journal</a><h1 class=\"text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight leading-[1.1] text-foreground mb-5\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">How to Choose a Microcement Installer in New Orleans (6-Question Checklist)</h1><div class=\"flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8\"><span class=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-calendar w-3.5 h-3.5\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M8 2v4\"></path><path d=\"M16 2v4\"></path><rect width=\"18\" height=\"18\" x=\"3\" y=\"4\" rx=\"2\"></rect><path d=\"M3 10h18\"></path></svg>February 15, 2026</span><span class=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-clock w-3.5 h-3.5\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"></circle><polyline points=\"12 6 12 12 16 14\"></polyline></svg>7 min read</span><span>by Ryan Mena</span></div><div class=\"aspect-[16/9] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl mb-10 bg-muted\"><img alt=\"How to Choose a Microcement Installer in New Orleans (6-Question Checklist) — The Shirtless Handyman, New Orleans\" class=\"w-full h-full object-cover\" src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1587023705112-34a9b4fe8317?w=1600&amp;fit=crop&amp;fm=jpg&amp;q=85\"></div><div class=\"space-y-5\"><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Microcement looks effortless when it's done right. A glass-smooth, jointless shower wall or a continuous floor that flows from kitchen to living room — that's the finish that gets shared on Instagram. What people don't see is the prep, the cure schedule, the sealer chemistry, and the trowel discipline that takes years to develop. Microcement is unforgiving — a bad installer can crack a finish in six months.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">So before you hire a microcement installer in New Orleans, work through these six questions. Any installer worth your money will answer them clearly and without dodging.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">1. \"Will you personally install this, or will it be a subcontractor?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Most renovation calls in New Orleans get sub-contracted out — the person you meet at the estimate is rarely the person who shows up with the trowel. Microcement is too specialized for that. Ask directly: who is on the trowel? If the answer involves a crew you've never met, walk.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Single-craftsman shops are the safest bet for microcement. You get one accountable person, same hands from estimate to final seal, and one phone number if something needs attention later.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">2. \"What microcement system and brand do you use?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">There's no such thing as generic microcement. Real microcement is a multi-component system from a specific manufacturer — Italian brands like Topciment, Ideal Work, and Pavigres are the European originals, and there are a handful of high-quality US distributors. The brand affects bonding, color stability, sealer compatibility, and warranty.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If your installer can't name the exact brand and system they're applying — or says \"it's the same as concrete overlay,\" — they're either using a watered-down knock-off or improvising. Both are bad news for a 10-year finish.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">3. \"Can I see a finished project in person, in my zip code?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Photos lie. Real microcement looks slightly different in person — the slight tonal shifts, the matte sheen, the way light moves across a hand-troweled surface. Insist on seeing a real installation in person before signing.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If your installer can't show you a finished project locally — meaning within a 30-minute drive of your home in New Orleans — that's a sign they don't have a local portfolio yet. That doesn't mean they're bad, but it means you're paying for their learning curve.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">4. \"How do you prep over existing tile or substrate?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">This is the question that separates the pros from the YouTube-trained. Microcement bonds to almost any substrate — tile, concrete, drywall, even properly-prepped wood — but only if the prep is done right. Listen for these phrases:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-base text-foreground/85\"><li>Mechanical scarification (scoring the surface) of existing tile</li><li>Cleaning with a degreaser and rinsing thoroughly</li><li>Applying a quartz-loaded bonding primer (not just a regular primer)</li><li>Allowing 24 hours of cure time before the first microcement coat</li><li>Reinforcement mesh embedded in the first coat (for floor installs especially)</li></ul><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If the installer says \"we just clean it and start troweling,\" walk away. That's how you get delamination in two years.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">5. \"Can I see past work, and the layers that went into it?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Photos of a fresh install don't show how the shower was built. Ask to see past work and have them name the layers: the substrate, the waterproofing, and the finish.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Ask specifically about the drain and the pan. If they can't point to those details on a finished shower, they haven't done this enough times.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">6. \"How is this shower waterproofed, and what about the drain and the pan?\"</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">Ask how the shower is waterproofed, ask to see past work and the layers used, and ask about the drain and the pan. You want to hear, in plain language, what's under the finish, how the corners and niche are detailed, and what happens if the existing pan has failed.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If the installer won't show you a finished shower or won't explain the layers, walk. The waterproofing is the part you can't see once the finish is on.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">The red flags that should end the conversation</h2><ul class=\"list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-base text-foreground/85\"><li>Won't name the microcement brand</li><li>Can't show a finished installation in person</li><li>Won't explain the waterproofing, the layers, or the drain and pan</li><li>Asks for more than 50% deposit</li><li>Says \"any handyman can do this\"</li></ul><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If you spot two or more of these, walk away — even if the quote looks attractive. The cost of a failed microcement install is 3–4x the cost of doing it right the first time, because demolition becomes the only path to fix.</p><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light tracking-tight text-foreground mt-12 mb-4 leading-snug\" style=\"font-family: Fraunces, serif;\">How we stack up against the checklist</h2><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">I built The Shirtless Handyman around exactly this checklist. I personally install every microcement project. I use Italian-imported systems. I'll walk you through every layer of the waterproofing — including the drain and the pan — before you sign anything.</p><p class=\"text-base text-foreground/85 leading-relaxed\">If you want to compare quotes — and we recommend you do — use this checklist as your scorecard. Any installer who passes it is worth talking to. If you'd like to start with us, see our microcement service page below.</p><div class=\"my-8 p-6 bg-[#1A3C34] text-white rounded-2xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between\"><p class=\"font-medium\">See the microcement installer page — book a free in-home walkthrough</p><a href=\"/microcement-installers-new-orleans\" class=\"inline-flex items-center h-11 px-6 rounded-full bg-[#D97757] text-white font-medium hover:bg-[#C56545] whitespace-nowrap no-underline\" data-testid=\"blog-post-cta-link\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-arrow-ri